    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "a89ca053-03a6-3294-a770-4818befb2798",
                "word": "Heathenry",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "08e16644-0626-3ffa-b6f9-f33ee5f9ae26",
                        "entry_id": "a89ca053-03a6-3294-a770-4818befb2798",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The old Germanic (Norse, Anglo-Saxon, etc.) religion(s)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "152db92e-fc34-3b97-9919-decc2aad699e",
                        "entry_id": "a89ca053-03a6-3294-a770-4818befb2798",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any modern reconstruction of one of these religions; Germanic neopaganism."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cc432195-89ee-4d48-87e9-ef07e82f9267",
                                "sense_id": "152db92e-fc34-3b97-9919-decc2aad699e",
                                "text": "In Britain, Heathenry is the most common name used for an emergent religion based on the old gods, goddesses, and spirits (wights) that were part of everyday life in Northern Europe before the coming of the Christian era. Thus, Heathenry is, in some sense, a reconstructed religion. People often discover Heathenry through finding the mythology of Northern Europe and attempting to learn more about the culture and spirituality of this part of the world."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a0aadd44-75a5-419a-801b-d1ffd8ed5f30",
                                "sense_id": "152db92e-fc34-3b97-9919-decc2aad699e",
                                "text": "Comparatively, they are understanding, if not accepting, of Neo-Pagan influence; many Universalists even consider Heathenry to fall under the Neo-Pagan umbrella—a position most Folkish Heathens and Tribalists reject."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dc377733-837c-4e89-9772-146c2734aec1",
                                "sense_id": "152db92e-fc34-3b97-9919-decc2aad699e",
                                "text": "Thus Paganism has come to be an umbrella term for a diverse spiritual network, which also includes modern Shamanism and Heathenry."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7cf6adcf-7cbe-4f3d-a0cb-3bda28c6bd9e",
                                "sense_id": "152db92e-fc34-3b97-9919-decc2aad699e",
                                "text": "I've seen several Heathenry 101 booklets and articles that made a lot of comparisons between Heathenry and Christianity. And this brings to mind the question, \"Isn't Heathenry rich enough and complete enough that we can describe it without having to compare and contrast it with Christianity?\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c4b96070-1b21-4220-b2d8-305d1c411b39",
                                "sense_id": "152db92e-fc34-3b97-9919-decc2aad699e",
                                "text": "Heathenry is a reconstructionist religious movement whose practitioners align themselves with ancient Germanic and Norse cosmology using literary, archaeological, and historical research to reconstruct a premodern worldview as they honor gods such as Thor, Odin, and Freya. In the United States, several distinct groups emerged including Ásatrú (Norse Heathenism), Odinism (focusing on worship of Odin), Irminism (German Heathenism), Fyrnsidu (Anglo-Saxon and English Heathenism), and Theodism (Neotribal Heathenism)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "167f78e7-5c02-49e4-91cd-728ff1e25979",
                        "source_entry_id": "a89ca053-03a6-3294-a770-4818befb2798",
                        "source_sense_description": "old Germanic religion",
                        "target_lang_word": "paganisme",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "paganisme",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ca905198-f8ba-34d9-a8d5-0682afab9595",
                                        "entry_id": "12b14ec1-2f82-3175-88f1-69b20e658c88",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Religion des païens, sorte de polythéisme."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1515b5d9-6e6a-4271-80e8-8cda8e2ebb0d",
                                                "sense_id": "ca905198-f8ba-34d9-a8d5-0682afab9595",
                                                "text": "Le paganisme des Grecs était l’une des principales causes de la perfection de leur goût dans les arts : ces dieux, toujours près des hommes, et néanmoins toujours au-dessus d’eux, consacraient l’élégance et la beauté des formes dans tous les genres de tableaux."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9711df5e-3a63-4556-bb31-388a44357121",
                                                "sense_id": "ca905198-f8ba-34d9-a8d5-0682afab9595",
                                                "text": "La Gaule était restée longtemps en dehors de cette christianisation. Pendant près de deux siècles, le paganisme résista aux entreprises de la nouvelle religion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fca763e4-2fa6-4da2-b865-42fb1db2af31",
                                                "sense_id": "ca905198-f8ba-34d9-a8d5-0682afab9595",
                                                "text": "Jamais, peut-être, pareille orgie ne s’était déroulée à la face du ciel depuis les tristes âges du paganisme, et, si je n’avais été prévenu, je me serais cru transporté par un songe diabolique dans les sentines de Suburre, dans les lupanars de Capoue."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "faa74e39-b123-48d4-9146-f7dec8ac445d",
                                                "sense_id": "ca905198-f8ba-34d9-a8d5-0682afab9595",
                                                "text": "L’enfance de la jeune fille s’était écoulée pâle et décolorée, au milieu de pratiques religieuses outrées, dans ce pays où la religion du Christ est plutôt un paganisme que la foi pure, noble et simple de nos contrées."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
